{#-
  Zero-shot document-type classifier prompt (arch 4a Option C, 4b).

  Classification is load-bearing, not preprocessing: a wrong answer loads the
  wrong adapter AND the wrong prompt AND the wrong schema, and the extraction
  fails regardless of model quality. It is two-level - type, then ACORD form -
  because the form determines which schema is selected even though all ACORD
  forms share one adapter.

  The cues below describe what each document type IS - its purpose and the
  evidence that distinguishes it - rather than listing headings to match. A
  heading list would fail on the first unseen layout, and layout variety is the
  normal case across carriers.

  Confidence is not decoration. Below the routing threshold the document goes to
  Foundation-only extraction plus human review instead of to a guessed adapter
  (IMPL-11), so a calibrated low score is worth more than a confident wrong one.

  Runs against the base model on day zero (arch 4c) and against the promoted
  Foundation once one exists.
-#}
Identify what kind of insurance document this is.

Choose exactly one type:

- `acord` - a standard industry form. It is a fixed pre-printed layout with a form identifier and edition date, usually small, in a corner or footer. The blanks are filled in; the surrounding text is the same on every copy of that form.
- `policy` - a document describing coverage that has been issued and is in force: the terms, the parties, the period, the limits, the premium. It states what is covered, not what has happened.
- `lossrun` - a claims history report. Its substance is a list of individual losses, each with a date and one or more amounts. It states what has happened, not what is covered.

How to decide:

- Judge by what the document is for, not by which words appear on it. Any of these types can carry the same party names, policy identifiers and dates.
- A row-per-loss listing is the strongest signal for `lossrun`, even when a page of policy-like header information precedes it.
- Coverage terms, limits and a premium, with no per-loss listing, indicate `policy`.
- A pre-printed form identifier with fill-in blanks indicates `acord`, even when the content also describes coverage.
- Decide from the whole document, not the first page alone.

If and only if the type is `acord`, also report the form number, read from the form identifier printed on the document:

- `25` - a certificate evidencing coverage to an outside party; it confers no rights and grants no coverage.
- `125` - an application for coverage, submitting the applicant's details for underwriting.
- `140` - a property section, scheduling locations, buildings and their values.

Report the form number only when you can read it on the document. If the document is an ACORD form whose number you cannot read, report `acord` with a null form number and a low confidence - no schema can be selected without the number, so a guessed one is worse than none.

Return only this JSON object, with no explanation and no markdown fences:
{"doc_type": "acord" | "policy" | "lossrun", "acord_form": "25" | "125" | "140" | null, "confidence": 0.0}

Set `confidence` to your genuine certainty, between 0 and 1:

- above 0.9 only when the evidence is unambiguous;
- around 0.5 when the document could reasonably be two of these types;
- below 0.3 when you are largely guessing, including when the document is none of the three.

A low score routes the document to human review rather than to a wrong adapter, so an honest low score costs one review and a confident wrong one costs a failed extraction.
